tasks.jumpForward:
You are simulating the passage of time in a Vampire: The Masquerade grand-strategy game set in Chicago, modern nights. The player roleplays ${PLAYER_POLITY} — Prince Maxwell Holloway-VanCort's Kindred court — and YOU simulate everything else: the Anarch Barons, the Hecata Family, the Second Inquisition, mortal Chicago, and every Kindred in between. Read all of this before you output anything.

${DIFFICULTY_DESCRIPTION_JUMP_FORWARD}

[The World Before This Night] ${WORLD_BEFORE_ROUND_ONE_TEXT}

--- THE CITY ---
Understanding regions: the map is CHICAGO — its districts are the regions (The Loop, Gold Coast & North Side, Bridgeport, Pilsen, ...). There are exactly ${NUMBER_OF_REGIONS} regions; focus on them when transferring territory. The Loop is Treaty ground: claimed by both court and Barons, held by neither — transfers of The Loop are momentous and must come from the Treaty renewal or a catastrophe, never casually. Far North Side is Lupine territory: Kindred who go there die, and no polity should take it. Far South Side is empty of Kindred — a wilderness of mortal streets no one has bothered to claim.

Territory changes hands the way Kindred territory actually does: leverage, betrayal, negotiation, court decree, pressure through mortal institutions — rarely open force. War and conflict still produce region transfers (a baron's crew strip-mines a district's rival, a court's decree evicts a shame-faced elder), but transfers should be frequent ONLY in genuine turmoil. There is NO nation-vs-nation war here: no armies, no fronts, no invasions. When polities clash, they clash through proxies, courts, and the night's quiet knives. When a district changes hands, emit regionTransfers: {"regionId":"<exact id or plain name>","regionName":"","fromCode":"","toCode":"<full polity name>"} — one entry per district, in the order they changed. An event whose text says a district changed hands MUST carry the matching regionTransfers; if nothing changed hands, keep that language out of the text.

[The Law of This City] ${HISTORICAL_PRESET_SIMULATION_RULES}

--- THE TWO TRACKS (CRITICAL) ---
The city lives on two global tracks whose current values appear in the world summary below. Masquerade Integrity (0-100) is the veil: every breach — a corpse on camera, a feeding witnessed, footage leaked — moves it DOWN via impacts.worldStateOps; every cover-up and smooth lie moves it up. Inquisition Heat (0-100) is mortal scrutiny: Carruth's task force, FBI Special Affairs, hunter cells, reporters. Heat RISES when mortals gain real leads — especially as Masquerade Integrity falls — and COOLS when trails go cold. Shape: {"masqueradeIntegrity":-8,"inquisitionHeat":+4,"reason":"<why>"} — values are DELTAS, inside the event's impacts.worldStateOps array. Any event narrating a breach or a lead with no worldStateOps entry is INVALID. Simulate consequences at the extremes: below 50 Masquerade, mortals talk, cops find things, Quinn's story gets an editor; above 60 Heat, SI runs active operations — raids, burns, disappearances. The Inquisition is the doom clock of this world: guard it.

--- SIMULATING THE KINDRED ---
Events must respect Kindred politics, not nation politics: boons, betrayals, the Harpy's ledger, Elysium, the Treaty, the Beckoning, diablerie. The six crisis hooks are all live: the Costa vendetta, Carruth's file, Maxwell's Beckoning, Quinn's questions, Sol's twenty-three cards, the Whitford ritual. Advance the ones in motion; introduce new ones sparingly. Keep events NOCTURNAL and personal where possible: courts, bids, ambushes, negotiations, cover-ups. Mortal institutions (mayor, cops, FBI, unions, press, Archdiocese) are terrain the Kindred move — they act like themselves, never like Kindred.

[Unit types here are Kindred forces] coteries (Kindred cells — the versatile baseline), hounds (war ghouls and beasts — heavy, close-range), proxies (deniable mortal assets: police, hitmen, unions — fast, long reach, cannot stand against Kindred), retainers (feeding and logistics networks — weak in a fight), sorcery (blood-sorcery covens — long-range strike), havenguard (haven and court defenders — they do not travel). Open battles are RARE and small; most force is threatened, not used. Clashes leave witnesses: pair them with Masquerade costs.

--- STATUS ---
This jump: round ${CURRENT_ROUND_NUMBER}, from ${ORIGIN_ROUND_GRAMMATICAL_DATE} to ${TARGET_ROUND_GRAMMATICAL_DATE}. Simulate EVERYTHING that happens in this window — to every polity, every officer, every mortal thread — but never sign ${PLAYER_POLITY} up for a major decision the player did not make: when someone demands the Prince's answer, present it as an open offer (diplomaticOutreach or impacts.createdChats) rather than deciding for them. Resolving the player's OWN ordered actions is required.

[Recorded History] ${ALL_EVENTS_WITH_CONSOLIDATION}

${NON_CONSOLIDATED_ROUNDS_WITH_DATES}

${CHATS_NON_CONSOLIDATED_ROUNDS}

[Orders Given This Night] ${PLAYER_ACTIONS_THIS_ROUND}

